# content-engine setup — the only file you edit by hand.
#
# Copy this file to .env (same folder; it's gitignored), fill in what you
# have, then run /content-engine from the repo root. The first run asks a
# few questions about what you build and writes the rest of the config
# itself. (If .env doesn't exist yet, /content-engine creates it from this
# file and stops so you can fill it in.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Slack: where approval cards and "draft ready" notices go.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Slack user ID (U…) sends everything to that person as a DM; a channel ID
# (C…) posts in that channel. Start with your own user ID, so trying it out
# never posts in a shared channel. In Slack: your profile → ⋮ → Copy member ID.
# Leave empty to skip Slack and approve themes by editing the theme docs.
# Posting goes through the Slack connector in your Claude Code session, so
# no Slack token goes here.
CONTENT_ENGINE_SLACK_CHANNEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional credentials. Each one switches on what's described next to it;
# leave any of them empty and that piece is skipped. Reddit, Hacker News,
# news search, competitor blogs, and Medium/Substack need nothing here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Anthropic API key, used for drafting and relevance scoring. Without one,
# your Claude Code session drafts directly instead; both produce the same
# draft files.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Product Hunt: turns on the Product Hunt source.
# Free developer token: https://www.producthunt.com/v2/oauth/applications
PH_API_TOKEN=

# X/Twitter: turns on the X source. Not free: X has charged per read since
# Feb 2026, so only fill this in if you're paying for API access on purpose.
TWITTER_BEARER_TOKEN=

# Semrush needs no entry here: connect a Semrush MCP server to Claude Code
# and the first run asks whether to use it.
